  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAt the time of this writing, thunderstorms have erupted over eastern\nIndiana along a convergence axis associated with the 850 mb theta-e \nridge. This theta-e gradient will attempt to shift eastward into\ndaybreak, but it will be competing against the thunderstorm growth \nthat will resist the movement east. PWATs are not incredibly high, \nbut they are still anomalous with values around 1.6\" advecting into \nthis corridor. Southeast Indiana and southwest Ohio have received \nsome rain from the previous rounds of thunderstorms, but flooding is \nnot actively ongoing. This will require multiple thunderstorms to \nmove through a localized area to elevate the potential for flash \nflooding. The most recent SPC HREF LPMM suggests rainfall maximums \ncould reach between 4-6\" locally. If the band can slip slowly \neastward over the next 3-5 hours, this should limit the more \nsignificant flash flood potential that materialize if the line \nremains stagnant. \n\nAfter daybreak, plentiful heating will take place over the cold pool\nair mass currently laid out over the entire Ohio Valley. Surface\nbased instability values will respond, and will become supportive of\ndiurnally driven thunderstorm development. The band of thunderstorms\novernight likely determine the eastward extent of the best heating\nfor afternoon thunderstorms. A shortwave dropping through the lower\nMidwest initiates thunderstorms over Illinois and Indiana by the \nlate morning. Through the afternoon, these thunderstorms will drop \nsouth- southeastward, merging with the remainder thunderstorms \nlingering from this morning. Effective bulk shear is a bit lower \ntoday when compared to yesterday's significant damaging wind \noutbreak. Large hail and damaging winds are still a threat, but the \nhigher wind values will be less widespread. Due to the location of \nthe morning storms, confidence is high that the severe threat would \nbe favored over southeast Indiana, southwest Ohio, and the western \nportion of northern Kentucky. If these mornings storms fail to move \neast, the flash flood threat would be higher but the severe threat \nwould trend lower. \n\nThe flood watch was extended through the evening today because the\nshortwave could still bring locally heavy rainfall over saturated\nground over central Ohio. The potential is lower, but it is not zero. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nAfter Wednesday's weather event ends, additional rainfall remains in\nthe forecast into Thursday and Friday. Additional weak shortwaves \nwithin the persistent northwesterly upper level flow will provide \nthe potential for additional clusters of thunderstorms and periods of\nheavy rain. The focus for the heaviest activity is expected to be \nfarther south as the front moves through Wednesday night. Therefore, \nthe severe threat is likely lower even with thunderstorms in the \nforecast. Temperatures will also be cooler with area highs ranging \nfrom the low to mid 80s. \n\nGoing into the weekend, the ridge builds northward again, pushing all\nthe way into the upper Midwest. Temperatures will increase as a\nresult with 90s returning. Cannot rule out lower chances for\nthunderstorms on Saturday as the front lifts back to the north,\nhowever coverage should remain scattered due to the lack of better\nupper level forcing. That changes moving into Sunday.\n\nThe next trough pushes into southeast Canada Sunday night into\nMonday. Bulk shear values around 25 knots and significant amounts of\nCAPE suggest there will be a renewed chance for severe weather. Drier\nair would then filter in behind the front Monday and Tuesday.",
